title: event-stream / npm 2018
page: breaches/event-stream-npm.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: In 2018, event-stream's maintainer handed publish rights to a volunteer who asked, whose new dependency carried an encrypted payload aimed at one Bitcoin wallet app.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: ~2M | weekly downloads | roughly two million, at the time
stat: None | compromise in the handover | handed over on request: no compromise, no theft
stat: 1 | wallet app targeted | only wallets above roughly 100 BTC or 1,000 BCH
stat: ~6.5 | weeks, injection to discovery | October 5 to November 20, 2018
The page calls this the oldest supply-chain kill chain on the site, and the template for several of the newest.
notes:
Start with the four figures the page leads with.

event-stream was an npm package doing roughly two million downloads a week, which its maintainer had last released in 2016. npm is the default package registry for JavaScript and Node.js. In September 2018 he handed publish rights over to a volunteer who asked for them. The page stresses that the handover itself involved no compromise and no theft.

The payload was precision-targeted: one wallet application, Copay, and within it only wallets above roughly 100 BTC or 1,000 BCH. BTC is bitcoin and BCH is Bitcoin Cash.

About six and a half weeks passed from injection on October 5, in flatmap-stream 0.1.1, to discovery on November 20, 2018.

The page calls this the oldest supply-chain kill chain on the site and the template for several of the newest.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: 2016 | Dominic Tarr, its author, last releases event-stream
event: September 2018 | Tarr hands publish rights to right9ctrl, a volunteer who asked
event: September 9, 2018 | Version 3.3.6 adds a new dependency
event: October 5, 2018 | The payload is injected, in the new dependency's version 0.1.1
event: November 20, 2018 | A developer investigating a deprecation warning finds it
event: Afterwards | npm removes the malicious package and version, and takes ownership
The page gives no date for npm's removal, or for the Copay releases that carried the code. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives these dates, and the timeline shows only those.

Dominic Tarr, who wrote event-stream, last released it in 2016. In September 2018 he transferred publish rights to a volunteer, using the handle right9ctrl, who asked for them. On September 9, 2018, version 3.3.6 added flatmap-stream as a dependency. The page dates the injection of the payload to October 5, 2018, in flatmap-stream 0.1.1. It was found on November 20, 2018, by a developer investigating a deprecation warning. That developer noticed the unfamiliar dependency and opened GitHub issue #116.

npm then removed flatmap-stream and the affected version and took ownership of event-stream. The page gives no date for that, or for Copay's versions 5.0.2 through 5.1.0, which Copay confirmed carried the malicious code.

The markers are evenly spaced, and the slide says the gaps between them are not to scale.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | Rights handed over on request | T1684.001
step: 02 | Supply Chain | flatmap-stream added | T1195.001
step: 03 | Stealth | Payload stays encrypted | T1027
step: 04 | Impact | One wallet app targeted | T1657
step: 05 | Discovery | A deprecation warning | -
step: 06 | Response | npm takes it back | -
Arrows follow the page's stage order. Stages 05 and 06 carry no ATT&CK technique.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's six stages, each with its phase and, where the page gives one, the first ATT&CK technique it lists.

Stage 01, Initial Access, is the handover of publish rights to a volunteer who asked. Stage 02, under the page's Supply Chain phase, is version 3.3.6 adding flatmap-stream as a dependency. Stage 03, under Stealth, is the payload staying encrypted, keyed to the consuming package's description. Stage 04, Impact, is the theft aimed at one wallet application, through its build.

Stage 05, Discovery, and stage 06, Response, carry no ATT&CK technique. They are a developer's curiosity about a deprecation warning, and npm taking the package back.

The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Tarr handed publish rights to right9ctrl, a volunteer who asked
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1684.001 | Social Engineering: Impersonation
technique: T1195.002 | Compromise Software Supply Chain
fact: Maintainer | had not used it for years, by his own account; unpaid, still fielding issues
fact: Compromise involved | none: no credential stolen, no system exploited
fact: Repeated in | XZ Utils, 2022 to 2024: same route, plus manufactured pressure
The handover transferred write access to a substantial share of the JavaScript ecosystem on the strength of a friendly request.
notes:
Stage 01 is the way in. No credential was stolen and no system was exploited. The page maps it to T1684.001, Social Engineering: Impersonation, and T1195.002, Compromise Software Supply Chain.

Dominic Tarr had written event-stream years earlier, had not used it for years, and kept receiving issues and requests for a package he had no personal stake in. Someone offered to help, and he did what the page says the open-source ecosystem asks maintainers to do in that situation: he handed it over. The volunteer used the handle right9ctrl.

The package still served around two million downloads a week, so the handover transferred write access to a substantial share of the JavaScript ecosystem on the strength of a friendly request.

The page calls this the template. The same route was taken again, far more patiently, against XZ Utils, where the backdoor surfaced in 2024. In that case sock puppets manufactured the pressure that made handover feel necessary. A sock puppet is a fake identity that one person runs while presenting it as someone else.

== On September 9, 2018, version 3.3.6 added flatmap-stream as a dependency
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Supply Chain
technique: T1195.001 | Compromise Software Dependencies and Development Tools
fact: flatmap-stream | a package created for this purpose
fact: Payload | in the new dependency, not in event-stream's own source
fact: Consumer visibility | effectively none: it arrived transitively
fact: Later releases | legitimate, further normalizing the package's activity
The change that mattered was a single line in a manifest.
notes:
On September 9, 2018, event-stream version 3.3.6 added flatmap-stream as a dependency, a package created for this purpose. The page maps this to T1195.001, which ATT&CK names Compromise Software Dependencies and Development Tools.

The malicious code was never in event-stream itself. The new dependency carried the payload, so anyone auditing event-stream's own source found nothing wrong, and the change that mattered was a single line in a manifest. A manifest is the file where a package declares its dependencies.

Adding a dependency is among the most routine things a maintainer does and among the least scrutinized by consumers. Most of them never saw the change at all, because it arrived transitively, several hops down a tree they had no reason to inspect. A transitive dependency is one a project pulls in through another package rather than by naming it. Legitimate later versions were also released, which the page says further normalized the package's activity. The page dates the payload's injection itself to October 5, 2018, in flatmap-stream 0.1.1.

The next slide explains what T1195.001 covers, in general terms.

== What ATT&CK's T1195.001 covers
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1195.001 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1195/001/
ATT&CK describes adversaries manipulating software dependencies and development tools before a final consumer receives them. Popular open source projects used as dependencies in many applications, such as npm packages, may be targeted to add malicious code.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

T1195.001 is a sub-technique of Supply Chain Compromise. ATT&CK says adversaries may manipulate software dependencies and development tools prior to receipt by a final consumer, for the purpose of data or system compromise.

ATT&CK adds that applications often depend on external software to function properly, and that popular open source projects that are used as dependencies in many applications, such as pip and npm packages, may be targeted as a means to add malicious code to users of the dependency.

== The payload stayed encrypted, keyed to the consuming package's description
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Stealth
technique: T1027 | Obfuscated Files or Information
technique: T1059.007 | Command and Scripting Interpreter: JavaScript
fact: Storage | encrypted data in a file disguised as a test fixture
fact: Decryption key | the description field of the consuming package
fact: Effect | the payload only decrypts inside the intended victim's build
fact: Analysis elsewhere | an opaque blob that decrypts to nothing
That is targeting enforced by cryptography rather than by a conditional statement.
notes:
Stage 03 is how the payload hid. The page maps it to T1027, Obfuscated Files or Information, and T1059.007, JavaScript.

The malicious logic sat encrypted in a file dressed as a test fixture, a file of sample data that a project's tests read. The decryption key was the description field of the package that would consume it. So the payload only decrypted successfully inside the intended victim's build, and anyone examining the code elsewhere saw an opaque blob that decrypted to nothing.

The page says the construction is elegant in a way worth appreciating for what it defeats. Targeting is enforced by cryptography rather than by a conditional statement, which makes analysis outside the target environment close to useless.

The page notes how directly this prefigures XZ Utils, where the payload also hid in files presenting as binary test fixtures.

In the defense matrix, no defense marks this stage. That gap is one of the discussion questions at the end. The next slide explains T1027 in general terms.

== What ATT&CK's T1027 covers
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1027 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1027/
credit: Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1059.007 (attack.mitre.org)
ATT&CK describes adversaries making a file difficult to discover or analyze by encrypting, encoding or otherwise obfuscating its contents. Payloads may be compressed, archived or encrypted to avoid detection.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

T1027, Obfuscated Files or Information, covers adversaries attempting to make an executable or file difficult to discover or analyze by encrypting, encoding or otherwise obfuscating its contents, on the system or in transit. ATT&CK calls this common behavior across platforms. Payloads may be compressed, archived or encrypted to avoid detection, and portions of files can be encoded to hide the plain-text strings that would otherwise help defenders with discovery.

ATT&CK's entry for JavaScript, T1059.007, adds that because such payloads are text-based, it is very common for adversaries to obfuscate their content.

Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1059.007: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/007/

== In Copay's release build, the payload planted code in the shipped app
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Impact
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
technique: T1555 | Credentials from Password Stores
fact: Release build | final stage written into a file in another dependency
fact: Users' devices | account data and private keys sent to an external server
fact: Threshold | wallets holding more than ~100 BTC or ~1,000 BCH
fact: Copay | a Bitcoin wallet app; 5.0.2 through 5.1.0 affected, per Copay
Precision targeting made millions of collateral installs the attacker's camouflage.
notes:
Stage 04 is the theft. The page maps it to T1657, Financial Theft, and T1555, Credentials from Password Stores.

Millions of installations, and the attacker wanted exactly one application: Copay, a Bitcoin wallet application. During Copay's release build, the payload wrote its final stage into a file in another dependency, one the build scripts would package into the app. On users' devices, that final stage checked balances and sent account data and private keys to an external server, only from wallets above roughly 100 BTC or 1,000 BCH. BTC is bitcoin and BCH is Bitcoin Cash. The page says it deliberately ignored smaller holdings to reduce the chance of anyone noticing: fewer victims noticing means longer dwell time. Copay confirmed the malicious code shipped in its versions 5.0.2 through 5.1.0.

The page calls the strategic point uncomfortable. Precision targeting makes the enormous population of collateral installs the camouflage for the attacker's plan. It also means "we use that package but we're not the target" is true right up until someone else's payload has a broader trigger.

The page does not say how much was stolen, so do not put a figure on it. The next slide explains dwell time and T1657 in general terms.

== What dwell time means, and what T1657 covers
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, Dwell Time | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-dwell-time
credit: Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1657 (attack.mitre.org)
Dwell time is how long an attacker is in your environment before detection, the metric defenders try to shrink. ATT&CK's T1657 covers adversaries stealing monetary resources from targets for their own financial gain.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary and MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

The glossary defines dwell time as how long an attacker is in your environment before detection, and calls it the metric defenders try to shrink.

ATT&CK's T1657, Financial Theft, covers adversaries stealing monetary resources from targets through extortion, social engineering, technical theft or other methods aimed at their own financial gain, at the expense of the availability of those resources for victims.

Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1657: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1657/

== A developer investigating a deprecation warning opened GitHub issue #116
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Discovery
fact: Injected | October 5, 2018, in flatmap-stream 0.1.1
fact: Discovered | November 20, 2018, about six and a half weeks later
fact: Noticed | an unfamiliar dependency
fact: Venue | GitHub issue #116, "I don't know what to say."
About six and a half weeks passed between injection and discovery, and it ended because one person was curious about something minor.
notes:
Stage 05 is the discovery, and the page maps it to no ATT&CK technique.

About six and a half weeks passed between injection on October 5, in flatmap-stream 0.1.1, and discovery on November 20, 2018. It ended because one person was curious about something minor. A developer noticed an unfamiliar dependency while looking into a deprecation notice, opened GitHub issue #116, and the encrypted payload unravelled from there. A deprecation warning is a notice that a package or feature is no longer recommended.

The thread that followed was titled "I don't know what to say." The page calls it one of the more consequential documents in open-source security, for Tarr's response to the anger directed at him rather than for the technical analysis. The next slide sets out that response.

== The argument in issue #116, unanswered since, per the page
layout: text
The page calls Tarr's reply the sharpest statement of the sustainability problem.
- He gave away a package he no longer used, for free.
- People who had taken his work without payment blamed him for not guarding it indefinitely.
notes:
The page's funding defense starts from this argument.

In the thread, Tarr pointed out that he had published the package for free, stopped using it years earlier, and was being blamed by people who had taken his work without payment and without contributing, for failing to keep guarding it indefinitely. The page's summary puts it this way: he had given away a package he no longer used, for free, and owed nobody anything.

The page says the argument has not been answered since. It calls it the same condition XZ Utils exploited in 2024, and the reason maintainer funding shows up as a security control on csoh.org rather than as a goodwill gesture.

== npm took ownership, since Tarr no longer had rights to fix it
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Response
fact: Removed | flatmap-stream and the affected event-stream version
fact: Ownership | npm assumed ownership of event-stream
fact: Tarr | could not act: he had already transferred publish rights
fact: Guidance | run npm audit and update
fact: Structural point | handover is irreversible from the transferring side
Handing a package over effectively means trusting the recipient permanently.
notes:
Stage 06 is the response, and the page maps it to no ATT&CK technique.

The page calls this a detail that catches people out. When Tarr was told about the backdoor, he could not remove it. He had transferred publish rights, so he had no more authority over the package than any other user, and had to ask npm to intervene. npm removed flatmap-stream and the affected version, took ownership of event-stream itself, and advised users to run npm audit and update. npm audit asks the registry for a report of known vulnerabilities in a project's dependencies.

The page draws the structural point: transferring maintainership is irreversible from the transferring side. So the decision to hand a package over is effectively a decision to trust the recipient permanently, made at a moment when the maintainer is usually tired and relieved that someone volunteered.

In the defense matrix, no defense marks this stage either.

== The same route, taken again at XZ Utils
layout: compare
col: event-stream, 2018 | A volunteer asked, and the maintainer handed it over | The payload sat encrypted in a file disguised as a test fixture | Tarr's argument: free work, abandoned years earlier, guarded indefinitely by whom?
col: XZ Utils, 2022 to 2024 | The same route, far more patiently, with sock puppets manufacturing the pressure | The payload also hid in files presenting as binary test fixtures | The same condition, exploited in 2024
The page calls event-stream the earliest instance of the handover pattern and XZ Utils the most sophisticated.
notes:
This is the pattern the page draws, and it returns to it at stages 01, 03 and 05.

At stage 01, the same handover route was taken again, far more patiently, against XZ Utils, where the backdoor surfaced in 2024. In that case sock puppets manufactured the pressure that made handover feel necessary. The page's summary says the handover mechanism reappears in XZ Utils about four years later.

At stage 03, the page notes that XZ Utils' payload also hid in files presenting as binary test fixtures. At stage 05, it calls Tarr's unanswered argument the same condition XZ Utils exploited in 2024.

The page calls event-stream the earliest instance of the pattern and XZ Utils the most sophisticated. XZ Utils has its own kill chain on csoh.org, linked at the end of this deck. Every XZ Utils detail on this slide comes from the event-stream page.

== Six defenses, and the stages their words name
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Pin by hash, review new dependencies | 02
row: Watch maintainership and ownership changes | 01
row: Constrain dependency code during a build | 04
row: High-value assets: assume you're targeted | 04
row: Fund the maintainers | 01 | 05
row: Reduce the tree | 01
No defense marks stage 03 or stage 06.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against its six stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something that stage has. The marks are this deck's reading of those words; the page itself names no stage numbers.

Pinning by hash names stage 02: the malicious code arrived as one added line in a manifest, several hops down the tree. Watching for maintainership changes names stage 01's handover; the page calls event-stream the earliest instance of that pattern. Constraining what dependency code can reach during a build names stage 04: the build step needed write access to another package's files. Assuming, if you hold high-value assets, that you are the specific target also names stage 04: the payload ignored every install except one application, and within that, only wallets above a threshold.

Funding the maintainers names stage 01, Tarr handing the package on when someone offered, and stage 05, his argument, which the page calls the reason maintainer funding is a security control on csoh.org. Reducing the tree cites stage 01's facts: two million downloads a week of code its author had not used for years.

In the matrix, no defense marks stage 03, the encrypted payload, or stage 06, npm's response. That gap is the second discussion question.

== Make new dependencies visible, and watch who can publish
layout: compare
col: Pin and review (stage 02) | One added manifest line, several hops down the tree, carried the payload | Enforced lockfile integrity hashes: nothing enters your build unreviewed | Tooling that surfaces new transitive dependencies in a diff
col: Watch handovers (stage 01) | A package changing hands is a security-relevant event | It is usually visible in public metadata | For your critical path, alert on it rather than discover it afterwards
Per the page: event-stream is the earliest instance of the pattern, XZ Utils the most sophisticated.
notes:
Two defenses work on what enters your build, and on who can change it.

First, pin dependencies by hash and treat a new transitive dependency as a reviewable change. The malicious code arrived as one added line in a manifest, several hops down the tree. A lockfile with enforced integrity hashes means nothing enters your build unreviewed, and tooling that surfaces new transitive dependencies in a diff turns the change that mattered here into something a human sees. A lockfile records the exact version of every package a build installs, and can record a hash of each one.

Second, watch for maintainership and ownership changes in your dependency path. The page calls event-stream the earliest instance of this pattern and XZ Utils the most sophisticated. A package changing hands is a security-relevant event, it is usually visible in public metadata, and for the dependencies in your critical path it is worth alerting on rather than discovering afterwards.

== Constrain dependency code in the build, and assume you are the target
layout: compare
col: Build-time code (stage 04) | Neither --ignore-scripts nor build egress denial would have stopped it | The build step needed write access to another package's files | Tight library-level sandboxing "could have likely worked", per the systematic analysis
col: High-value assets (stage 04) | One app, and only wallets above a threshold | Treat the build pipeline as directly targeted infrastructure | Reproducible builds, human dependency review, builds hard-separated from key-holding systems
Per the page: no install script; the theft and upload ran later, on users' devices.
notes:
Two defenses answer stage 04.

First, constrain what dependency code can reach during a build. The page says no install script was involved: the payload ran when Copay's release build script loaded the module, so running installs with --ignore-scripts would not have stopped it. An install script is a command a package declares for the package manager to run when it is installed. Default-denying egress from build environments would not have stopped it either, because the build-time step decrypted the final stage and wrote it into a file in another dependency; the theft and the upload to an external server ran later, in the shipped app on users' devices. Egress is outbound traffic. What the build step did need was write access to another package's files. The systematic analysis judged that tight library-level sandboxing could have likely worked, as there was no reason for flatmap-stream's purely functional computation to access the file system.

Second, if you handle high-value assets, assume you are the specific target. The payload ignored every install except one application, and within that only wallets above a threshold. The page says organizations holding cryptocurrency, payment flows or similar should treat their build pipeline as directly targeted infrastructure: reproducible builds, dependency review by a human, and a hard separation between build systems and anything holding keys.

== Fund the maintainers, and shrink the tree
layout: compare
col: Fund maintainers (stages 01 and 05) | Tarr gave away free work and handed it on when someone offered | Fund the projects in your dependency path | Contribute maintenance, not only issues
col: Reduce the tree (stage 01) | Two million weekly downloads of code its author had not used for years | Audit whether each dependency still earns its place | Prefer the standard library or a small vendored function over a micro-package
The page calls funding the control that addresses the cause of both this chain and XZ Utils, and removing a dependency the only permanent mitigation.
notes:
The last two defenses are about the dependencies themselves and the people who maintain them.

Fund the maintainers, and understand why the page calls that a control and not a courtesy. Tarr's argument was never really rebutted: he gave away free work, stopped needing it, and handed it on when someone offered. Every organization depending on the package benefited from years of unpaid labour and contributed nothing to the moment it became a liability. In the page's words, funding the projects in your dependency path, and contributing maintenance rather than only issues, is the control that addresses the cause of both this chain and XZ Utils.

Reduce the tree, since every package is a trust decision that outlives its author's interest. event-stream was two million downloads a week of code its author had not used for years. Periodically audit whether each dependency still earns its place, prefer the standard library or a small vendored function over a micro-package, and remember that the page calls removing a dependency the only mitigation that is permanent. Vendoring means copying a dependency's code into your own repository.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Is every dependency pinned by hash, in a lockfile with enforced integrity?
check: Would a new transitive dependency show up in a diff a human reviews?
check: Would you be alerted if a package in your critical path changed hands?
check: Can dependency code in your builds write to other packages' files?
check: If you hold high-value assets, are build systems kept apart from anything holding keys?
check: Do you fund, or help maintain, the projects in your dependency path?
check: When did you last check that each dependency still earns its place?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether your dependencies are pinned by hash in a lockfile with enforced integrity, and whether a new transitive dependency would appear in a diff that a human reads.

Ask whether you would be alerted when a package in your critical path changes hands, since the page says that is usually visible in public metadata.

Ask what dependency code can reach during your builds, and whether it can write to another package's files, which is what this payload's build step needed. If you hold cryptocurrency, payment flows or similar, ask whether your build systems are hard-separated from anything holding keys, with reproducible builds and dependency review by a human.

Finally, ask whether you fund or help maintain the projects in your dependency path, and whether each dependency still earns its place.

== Would you know if a package in your critical path changed hands?
layout: question
event-stream changed hands on request, with no compromise. The volunteer who took it over added the dependency that carried the payload.

Name one package in your critical path: who can publish it today, and would you hear if that changed?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

event-stream changed hands on request. No credential was stolen and no system was exploited. The volunteer who took it over, right9ctrl, added the new dependency, flatmap-stream, that carried the payload.

Ask each person to name one specific package before you show the answer slide. The answer slide gives what the page says about handovers.

== What the page says about handovers
layout: answer
- A package changing hands is a security-relevant event, usually visible in public metadata.
- For your critical path, it is worth alerting on rather than discovering afterwards.
- Handover is irreversible from the transferring side; Tarr had to ask npm.
notes:
The page's answer is to watch for maintainership and ownership changes in your dependency path.

A package changing hands is a security-relevant event, it is usually visible in public metadata, and for the dependencies in your critical path the page says it is worth alerting on rather than discovering afterwards.

The page also makes a point about handovers at stage 06. Transferring maintainership is irreversible from the transferring side. When Tarr was told about the backdoor he could not remove it, because he had already transferred publish rights, and he had to ask npm to intervene.

Bring the group's examples back to this. If nobody would hear when a critical package changed hands, the page's defense applies.

== What would you add for stages 03 and 06?
layout: question
In the matrix, the page's defenses mark stages 01, 02, 04 and 05; none marks stage 03's encrypted payload or stage 06's response.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gaps in the defense matrix.

In the matrix, the page's defenses mark stages 01, 02, 04 and 05. None marks stage 03, where the payload stayed encrypted outside the intended victim's build, or to stage 06, where npm removed the malicious package and took ownership of event-stream.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== No defense marks stages 03 and 06
layout: answer
In the matrix, no defense marks these stages, and this deck adds none. Test any idea against reported details: a file disguised as a test fixture, a package description used as the key, npm taking ownership.
notes:
Be clear that the deck stops here. In the matrix, no defense marks stage 03 or stage 06, and this deck adds none of its own. Some proposals will apply the page's other defenses to these stages; that argument is the group's own.

If the group proposes ideas, treat them as the group's, and test each one against the facts the page reports.

For stage 03, those facts are the encrypted data in a file disguised as a test fixture, the decryption key taken from the description field of the consuming package, and an opaque blob that decrypts to nothing anywhere else.

For stage 06, they are npm removing flatmap-stream and the affected version, npm assuming ownership of event-stream, its advice to run npm audit and update, and the page's point that handover is irreversible from the transferring side.

== Sources
layout: links
link: npm, details about the event-stream incident | https://blog.npmjs.org/post/180565383195/details-about-the-event-stream-incident
